# Home Assistant writes its whole runtime state into the config directory,
# alongside the handful of yaml files that are actually ours. Everything is
# ignored by default and the files we own are re-added, so an HA upgrade that
# starts writing some new directory doesn't quietly land in a commit.
*
!.gitignore
!configuration.yaml
!automations.yaml
!scripts.yaml
!floorplan-demo.yaml

# For reference, what the ignore above is keeping out:
#   .storage/              the auth store, the UI dashboards, config entries
#   home-assistant_v2.db   the recorder -- this is the sample data, and it is
#                          regenerated by the automations, never committed
#   home-assistant.log
#   .HA_VERSION, deps/, tts/, backups/, image/
#   www/                   an empty mount point; the real files come from dist/
